<div class="page-head">
  <span class="eyebrow">Officers only</span>
  <h1>Update this website</h1>
  <p class="page-head__sub">
    Everything on this site — every headline, paragraph, photo, news item,
    officer and funding opportunity — is edited through a web form. You never
    touch code, and you cannot break the site.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="admin-launch">
  <div>
    <h2>Open the editor</h2>
    <p>
      Sign in with GitHub, pick the <strong>gssa-website</strong> repository,
      and you will see the whole site laid out as editable sections.
    </p>
    <a class="btn btn--primary" href="{{ admin.editorUrl }}" rel="noopener">
      Open the GSSA editor →
    </a>
  </div>
</div>

{% if admin.note %}
  <p class="ref" style="margin-top: 1rem;">{{ admin.note }}</p>
{% endif %}

<div class="callout" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
  <h3>Before you start: never publish private information here</h3>
  <p>
    This website is public, and so is the repository behind it. The
    <strong>membership roster, meeting minutes, officer applications, budgets
    and scoring records must never be entered here.</strong> They stay in the
    officers' SLU Google Workspace.
  </p>
  <p>
    Photos of people need that person's permission before you upload them.
  </p>
  <p class="ref">GSSA Bylaws Art. IX, Section 1 (9.3).</p>
</div>

<section class="section">
  <div class="section__head">
    <h2>What you can change</h2>
    <p class="section__note">
      Each of these is a section in the editor's left-hand menu.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="admin-tasks">
    <article class="admin-task">
      <h3>News</h3>
      <p class="admin-task__what">
        The dated list on the home page.
      </p>
      <p>
        Click <strong>News → Add an entry</strong>. Give it a title, pick the
        date, and write the announcement in the text box — it works like a
        normal document, with bold, links and lists. You can attach a photo and
        add “read more” links.
      </p>
      <p class="admin-task__tip">
        Newest items appear at the top automatically. To take something down,
        delete the entry.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="admin-task">
      <h3>Executive Board</h3>
      <p class="admin-task__what">
        The officer cards on the Executive Board page.
      </p>
      <p>
        Click <strong>Board Members</strong> to edit a card — name, how to
        pronounce it, role, program, photo, email and office hours. Drag a photo
        straight into the photo box; any shape works, because it is
        centre-cropped into a circle for you. Officers without a photo show a
        neutral circle, so the page still looks finished while you collect them.
        The bio sits behind a &ldquo;Read bio&rdquo; link, which keeps the grid
        tidy.
      </p>
      <p class="admin-task__tip">
        <strong>At the end of the year:</strong> add an entry for each new
        officer with the new term (for example <code>2027-2028</code>), then
        open <strong>Site Settings</strong> and change
        <strong>Current term</strong> to match. Last year's board moves into
        “Past Boards” by itself. Do not delete anyone — the archive is the
        record.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="admin-task">
      <h3>Funding &amp; Awards</h3>
      <p class="admin-task__what">
        Opportunities and their published terms.
      </p>
      <p>
        Click <strong>Funding Opportunities → Add an entry</strong>. Set the
        status to <em>Open</em>, <em>Coming soon</em> or <em>Closed</em>, then
        fill in eligibility, how many are available, how to apply, the deadline,
        when decisions come, and the selection criteria.
      </p>
      <p class="admin-task__tip">
        Anything you leave blank shows publicly as “To be published before
        applications open.” The bylaws require all six to be filled in
        <em>before</em> you start taking applications.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="admin-task">
      <h3>Recipients</h3>
      <p class="admin-task__what">
        The photo gallery of students who received an opportunity.
      </p>
      <p>
        Click <strong>Award Recipients → Add an entry</strong>. Add the
        student's name, which award, the term, a photo and a short note. Ask
        permission before posting anyone's photo or name.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="admin-task">
      <h3>Page text</h3>
      <p class="admin-task__what">
        The wording on every page.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Home Page</strong>, <strong>Board Page</strong>,
        <strong>Funding Page</strong> and <strong>Membership Page</strong> each
        contain the headings and paragraphs for that page. Rewrite anything you
        like. The “What we do” tiles on the home page are a list you can add to,
        remove from or reorder.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="admin-task">
      <h3>Site Settings</h3>
      <p class="admin-task__what">
        Things that apply everywhere.
      </p>
      <p>
        Contact email, social media links, the current term, and the
        <strong>membership form link</strong>.
      </p>
      <p class="admin-task__tip">
        The membership form link is the one the <strong>Membership Form</strong>
        button on the Membership page opens. It currently holds a placeholder
        address that goes nowhere — replace it the moment the real form exists.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="section__head"><h2>How saving works</h2></div>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li>
      <strong>You click Save in the editor.</strong> Your change is recorded
      with your name and the date.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The site rebuilds itself.</strong> This takes about a minute — no
      action needed from you.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Refresh the page to see it live.</strong> If it has not appeared
      after a few minutes, check the Actions tab in the repository.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <div class="callout callout--info">
    <h3>You cannot lose anything</h3>
    <p>
      Every save is stored permanently. If something is deleted or a page ends
      up looking wrong, any previous version can be restored — including work
      from officers who left years ago. Nothing you do in the editor is
      unrecoverable.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="section__head">
    <h2>Handing over to the next board</h2>
    <p class="section__note">
      Outgoing officers transfer access within ten business days of the end of
      their term — Bylaws Art. IX, 9.8.
    </p>
  </div>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li>
      <strong>Add the incoming officers</strong> to the GSSA GitHub
      organization, and give them the editor link on this page.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Add their board entries</strong> and update the current term, as
      described above.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Remove departing officers</strong> from the organization once the
      new board can sign in and save successfully.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Confirm the recovery email</strong> on the GitHub organization is
      a GSSA address, not any individual's personal account.
    </li>
  </ol>
</section>
